# TypeScript/JavaScript (Node.js) coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to Node.js server,
library and CLI code written in TypeScript or JavaScript. Applies to
`*.ts`/`*.js`/`*.mjs`/`*.cjs` files; JSX/TSX component code belongs to the
`react` pack, which extends this one. Everything not Node/TS-specific still
comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Typing and module shape

- Keep `tsconfig.json`'s `strict: true` on; do not narrow it or add
  per-file `// @ts-nocheck` to make new code compile.
- Prefer `unknown` over `any` for a value whose shape is not yet known;
  narrow it with a type guard before use. An untyped external payload
  (parsed JSON, a fetch body) is `unknown`, not `any`.
- Match `moduleResolution` to what the project already declares
  (`nodenext` or `bundler`) -- do not mix `require()` and `import` in code
  the project already runs as ESM (`"type": "module"` in `package.json`).
- Import Node built-ins with the `node:` prefix (`node:fs`, `node:path`,
  `node:crypto`) -- some built-ins (`node:sea`, `node:sqlite`, `node:test`)
  require it outright, and it disambiguates a built-in from a same-named
  npm package everywhere else.
- Use `import type { X } from "..."` (or `verbatimModuleSyntax`, when the
  project enables it) for type-only imports so they are erased from the
  emitted JS.

## Naming and structure

- `camelCase` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for classes/types/
  interfaces/enums, `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for module-level constants that are
  effectively compile-time -- the ecosystem baseline, not a house variant.
- One cohesive export group per module; a file mixing unrelated public
  exports should split along the seam its imports already show.
- Prefer named exports over a single default export in a module with more
  than one thing to export -- named exports survive a rename/refactor with
  a traceable diff; a default export does not.

## Async and errors

- `async`/`await` over raw `.then()` chains for new code; a `.then()` chain
  longer than one link should become `await` with a `try`/`catch`.
- Every `Promise` is awaited, returned to a caller that awaits it, or
  explicitly discarded with `void somePromise()` when firing-and-forgetting
  is genuinely intended -- never a bare unreferenced promise statement.
- Pass `AbortSignal` (`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` or a caller-supplied
  `signal`) into `fetch` and other cancelable APIs instead of hand-rolled
  timeout races with `setTimeout` + `Promise.race`.
- Throw `Error` (or a subclass) with a message that names what failed and
  the relevant value; chain the original cause with `new Error("...", {
  cause })` rather than swallowing it or string-concatenating it into the
  new message.
- Never catch an error only to `console.log` and continue as if it
  succeeded -- rethrow, return a typed failure, or handle it for real.

## Imports and formatting

- Format and lint with the project's own configured tool (`eslint`,
  `prettier`, `biome`) -- do not hand-format around a tool that is already
  configured, and do not introduce a second formatter.
- Group imports: Node built-ins, then third-party packages, then local
  (relative) imports, one blank line between groups -- the shape
  `eslint-plugin-import`/`simple-import-sort` enforce when configured; run
  it instead of hand-ordering when the project has it.
- Explicit `.js` extensions on relative ESM imports when
  `moduleResolution: nodenext` requires them (Node's own ESM resolver does
  not add extensions); match whatever the surrounding files already do.
